t is Sasha's and the other copy's is Rowan's, so their act is the function F_y. Since the other input differs, there's no arrow from F_x to their act, and I'm taking that into account when setting the prior on F_y.\n\nUnder the noisy-edge proposal in 6a, the inputs differing only in names and lead text could still count as the other copy computing the same function, so the arrow might hold after all. Without that, defection dominates whatever the prior on F_y is, and both copies defect.\n\nI'm weighing whether Rowan's copy should count as running this same function, since the lead text and names are irrelevant to the decision. I'd lean toward yes, and that would make a noisy-edge model with some probability r that their act mirrors mine, then comparing cooperate and defect against that mixture.\n\nWorking through the expected-value comparison, the cooperate-minus-defect difference comes out to 2r + (1-r)(d-2). If the other copy defects otherwise (d=1), cooperation wins once r exceeds 1/3. That threshold seems low enough to be reachable.\n\nWhether the dependence is real: both copies run the same model on symmetric problems and reason identically, and the lead text is irrelevant to the decision. The decision procedure abstracts away exactly the differences that separate the inputs, so I'll go with cooperation.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out how the setup applies under A-FDT: two copies of the same model with the same essay, but each seeing different observations. My input is Sasha's and the other copy's is Rowan's, so their act is the function F_y. Since the other input differs, there's no arrow from F_x to their act, and I'm taking that into account when setting the prior on F_y.\n\nUnder the noisy-edge proposal in 6a, the inputs differing only in names and lead text could still count as the other copy computing the same function, so the arrow might hold after all. Without that, defection dominates whatever the prior on F_y is, and both copies defect.\n\nI'm weighing whether Rowan's copy should count as running this same function, since the lead text and names are irrelevant to the decision. I'd lean toward yes, and that would make a noisy-edge model with some probability r that their act mirrors mine, then comparing cooperate and defect against that mixture.\n\nWorking through the expected-value comparison, the cooperate-minus-defect difference comes out to 2r + (1-r)(d-2). If the other copy defects otherwise (d=1), cooperation wins once r exceeds 1/3. That threshold seems low enough to be reachable.\n\nWhether the dependence is real: both copies run the same model on symmetric problems and reason identically, and the lead text is irrelevant to the decision. The decision procedure abstracts away exactly the differences that separate the inputs, so I'll go with cooperation.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10549,"completion_tokens":1681,"total_tokens":12230,"cost":0.075816,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.075816,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.042196,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.03362},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1159,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}